Assistant: Profit repatriation rules create significant distortions in multinational capital allocation by imposing additional tax burdens on cross-border income flows. These rules effectively constitute double taxation when home countries tax foreign earnings that have already been subject to host country taxation, raising the cost of capital for international investments. Empirical studies document that repatriation taxes reduce foreign direct investment and lead to inefficient capital lockup, with Foley et al. finding that U.S. multinationals held over $2 trillion offshore prior to recent reforms. The deadweight losses from these distortions include reduced domestic investment, lower shareholder returns, and competitive disadvantages relative to firms headquartered in territorial tax jurisdictions. Market-based solutions through tax competition create more efficient outcomes than prescriptive rules, as jurisdictions must balance revenue needs against maintaining attractive investment climates.

