Assistant: The targeting efficiency of fiscal instruments depends critically on minimizing deadweight losses from transfers to inframarginal recipients who would maintain consumption absent the transfer. Means-tested programs, despite administrative costs, generate substantially lower fiscal burdens per dollar of poverty reduction compared to universal schemes. The empirical literature on transfer programs demonstrates significant behavioral responses along both extensive and intensive labor supply margins, with income effects reducing work effort among near-poor households. Self-targeting mechanisms through work requirements or in-kind provision can improve allocative efficiency by screening out higher-ability individuals. Evidence from randomized evaluations shows that narrowly targeted conditional cash transfers achieve comparable poverty impacts at 20-30% of the fiscal cost of universal programs. Moreover, broad-based transfers create political economy distortions as middle-class capture diverts resources from the truly needy, while generating higher marginal tax rates to finance expanded programs.

